@meta
id: 15-06
title: Drill — keep one customer's rows
chapter: 15
type: drill
skills: global-invert, global-delete
par: 24
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: sort by customer, select the others, delete, fix the header you took with them
open: orders.csv

@brief
Eight times: support want the export cut down to customer `cust_44`.
Keep the header row and every `cust_44` row. Everything else goes.

The header does not contain `cust_44`. Say where the filtering starts.

@keys
| `:2,$v/pattern/d` | from line 2 to the end, delete every line not matching |

@hints
`:v` would take the header too. Start it on line 2.
--
`:2,$v/cust_44/d` and Enter.

@solution
    :2,$v/cust_44/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-one keystrokes. The range and the inverse are one idea now: name
the lines to keep, and name where the keeping starts.

CSV exports with a header are the everyday shape of this. Logs with a
banner line, SQL dumps with a preamble, markdown with a title: anything
where line 1 is special and the rest is data. `2,$` is the range you
will type for all of them.

@start orders.csv
id,customer_id,status,total
ord_4001,cust_10,PENDING,4999
ord_4002,cust_44,SHIPPED,12000
ord_4003,cust_11,PENDING,100
ord_4004,cust_44,CANCELLED,2599
ord_4005,cust_12,SHIPPED,7800
ord_4006,cust_44,SHIPPED,3100
ord_4007,cust_10,SHIPPED,4999
ord_4008,cust_13,PENDING,650
ord_4009,cust_44,PENDING,9900
ord_4010,cust_11,CANCELLED,100
ord_4011,cust_44,SHIPPED,1250
ord_4012,cust_12,SHIPPED,7800

@expected orders.csv
